If R has exactly two right ideals, this implies that the right ideals of R are {0} and R itself.

For R to have exactly two right ideals, it must be the case that R is either a field (division ring) or a ring with no nontrivial right ideals. This means every nonzero element of R has a multiplicative inverse (II) because otherwise, there would exist a nontrivial right ideal generated by a nonzero non-invertible element.

Additionally, since R has exactly two right ideals, it cannot be finite (III) because a finite ring can have more than two right ideals depending on its structure (for example, a finite integral domain is a field).

Therefore, R must be a division ring (II) and cannot be finite (III).

The statement that R is commutative (I) is not necessarily true; there are examples of division rings that are not commutative (such as the quaternions).

Thus, the correct answer is **H. III only**.
